or high dynamic range imaging and illumination correction. Currently, their work addresses the challenges of enhancing visibility in extreme low-light conditions and developing efficient spatio-temporal alignment frameworks.\n\n## Research Trajectory\n\nXiaoning Liu’s research began with a focus on ill-posed inverse problems in computational photography, specifically addressing the limitations of existing simulation datasets for real-world image dehazing. To address this, Liu introduced PASTA: Towards Flexible and Efficient HDR Imaging Via Progressively Aggregated Spatio-Temporal Aligment. This novel framework balances performance and latency by harnessing hierarchical representations and diverse granularities during feature disentanglement. This approach contributes to themes such as Hybrid CNN-Transformer Architectures for Global-Local Vision, seeking to capture long-range dependencies without the prohibitive computational costs of standard global attention.\n\nConcurrently, Liu took a prominent leadership role in the computer vision community’s efforts to benchmark and advance low-light image enhancement (LLIE). Serving as the first author on the NTIRE 2024 Challenge on Low Light Image Enhancement: Methods and Results, Liu coordinated an evaluation of state-of-the-art solutions from 22 competing teams. The challenge focused on generating clear, visually appealing images under extreme darkness, backlighting, and non-uniform illumination, particularly at ultra-high resolutions (4K and beyond). Furthermore, the drive toward processing high-resolution images efficiently—a theme established in Liu’s earlier HDR research—connects to the broader research direction of Linear Complexity Vision with State Space Models, reflecting an ongoing effort to overcome the quadratic scaling limitations of traditional vision architectures when processing large visual inputs.\n\nProfile updated July 5, 2026\n\n[Button: Papers (5)] [Button: Research Directions (11)] [Button: New Papers Nearby] [Button: Research Neighbors]\n\nTitle  | Venue  | Year\n--- | --- | ---\nNTIRE 2025 Challenge on Low Light Image Enhancement: Methods and Results  | Workshops @ CVPR  | 2025\nPASTA: Towards Flexible and Efficient HDR Imaging Via Progressively Aggregated Spatio-Temporal Aligment  | arXiv  | 2024\nNTIRE 2024 Challenge on Low Light Image Enhancement: Methods and Results  | Workshops @ CVPR  | 2024\nNTIRE 2024 Challenge on Low Light Image Enhancement: Methods and Results  | Workshops @ CVPR  | 2024\nJoint Contrast Enhancement and Exposure Fusion for Real-World Image Dehazing  | IEEE Trans. Multimedia  | 2022\n\nOther researchers whose papers cluster into the same research directions — a proxy for overlapping technical interests and shared intellectual neighborhoods.\n\nWenyi Lian 4 shared research directionsMingshen Wang 4 shared research directionsYuanbo Zhou 5 shared research directionsQinglong Yan 3 shared research directionsBo Wang 7 shared research directionsUma Mudenagudi 9 shared research directionsYu Gao 4 shared research directionsRoey Mechrez 3 shared research directions--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPASTA: Towards Flexible and Efficient HDR Imaging Via Progressively Aggregated Spatio-Temporal Alignment | Arxiv - DeepPaper (https://arxiv.deeppaper.ai/papers/2403.10376v2)\nciteturn34search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; PASTA: Towards Flexible and Efficient HDR Imaging Via Progressively Aggregated Spatio-Temporal Alignment ... Xiaoning Liu, Ao Li, Zongwei Wu, Yapeng Du, Le Zhang, Yulun Zhang, Radu Timofte, Ce Zhu\nOur approach achieves effectiveness and efficiency by harnessing hierarchical representation during feature distanglement. Through the utilization of diverse granularities within the hierarchical structure, our method substantially boosts computational speed and optimizes the HDR imaging workflow. In addition, we explore within-scale feature modeling with local and global attention, gradually merging and refining them in a coarse-to-fine fashion. Experimental results showcase PASTA's superiority over current SOTA methods in both visual quality and performance metrics, accompanied by a substantial 3-fold (x3) increase in inference speed.\n\n### Summary\n\nPASTA is a novel framework for HDR deghosting that achieves a 3x speedup over existing methods while maintaining state-of-the-art visual quality and performance by using progressively aggregated spatio-temporal alignment and hierarchical feature representation.\n\n### Subjects\n\ncs.CV\n\n### arXiv\n\nImage: arXiv 2403.10376v2\n\n### Domain\n\nTransformer Image Processing HDR Imaging\n\nAuthors\n\nXiaoning Liu, Ao Li, Zongwei Wu, Yapeng Du, Le Zhang, Yulun Zhang, Radu Timofte, Ce Zhu\n\nSubmitted\n\nMar 15, 2024--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Le Zhang 0001 (https://dblp.org/pid/03/4043-1.html)\nciteturn34search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Xiaoning Liu, Ao Li Image: 0000-0002-8866-2302, Zongwei Wu, Yapeng Du, Le Zhang, Yulun Zhang, Radu Timofte, Ce Zhu:\nPASTA: Towards Flexible and Efficient HDR Imaging Via Progressively Aggregated Spatio-Temporal Aligment.\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2403-10376\n\nXiaoning Liu, Ao LiImage: 0000-0002-8866-2302, Zongwei Wu, Yapeng Du, Le Zhang, Yulun Zhang, Radu Timofte, Ce Zhu:\nPASTA: Towards Flexible and Efficient HDR Imaging Via Progressively Aggregated Spatio-Temporal Aligment. CoRR abs/2403.10376 (2024)\n\n[i41]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n-----------------------------------------------