n with non-local sparse attention. In *IEEE/CVF Conf. on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)*, pages 3516–3525, 2021. 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8\n\n[39] Ben Niu, Weilei Wen, Wenqi Ren, Xiangde Zhang, Lianping Yang, Shuzhen Wang, Kaihao Zhang, Xiaochun Cao, and Haifeng Shen. Single image super-resolution via a holistic attention network. In *European Conf. Comp. Vis.ion (ECCV)*, page 191–207, 2020. 1, 2, 4\n\n6669--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPASTA: Towards Flexible and Efficient HDR Imaging Via Progressively Aggregated Spatio-Temporal Alignment | AI Research Paper Details (https://www.aimodels.fyi/papers/arxiv/pasta-towards-flexible-efficient-hdr-imaging-via)\nciteturn34search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; # PASTA: Towards Flexible and Efficient HDR Imaging Via Progressively Aggregated Spatio-Temporal Alignment ... Published 4/10/2024 by Xiaoning Liu, Ao Li, Zongwei Wu, Yapeng Du, LE ZHANG, Yulun Zhang, Radu Timofte\n\n# PASTA: Towards Flexible and Efficient HDR Imaging Via Progressively Aggregated Spatio-Temporal Alignment\n\nCan we make HDR imaging fast and efficient enough for everyday use, even on high-resolution images?\n\nPublished 4/10/2024 by Xiaoning Liu, Ao Li, Zongwei Wu, Yapeng Du, LE ZHANG, Yulun Zhang, Radu Timofte\n\n## Overview\n\n  * This paper introduces PASTA, a novel approach for efficient and flexible high dynamic range (HDR) imaging through progressively aggregated spatio-temporal alignment.\n  * The key ideas include a hierarchical representation of the HDR image and a progressive aggregation mechanism to align and fuse multi-exposure images.\n  * The proposed method aims to address the challenges of existing HDR imaging techniques, such as requiring specialized hardware, complex processing, and limited flexibility.\n\n## Plain English Explanation\n\nPASTA is a new way to create high-quality HDR (high dynamic range) images from multiple exposures, which are photos taken with different amounts of light. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCVPR 2025 Papers with Code & Data – Resources | Paper Digest (https://resources.paperdigest.org/2025/06/cvpr-2025-papers-with-code-data/)\nciteturn34search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Crawled: today; The IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR) is one of the top computer vision conferences in the world. ... To facilitate rapid community engagement with the presented research, we have compiled an extensive index of accepted papers that have associated public code or data repositories. ...  | Paper  | Author(s)  | Code ... 1  | Molmo and PixMo: Open Weights and Open Data for State-of-the-Art Vision-Language Models\n\n# CVPR 2025 Papers with Code & Data\n\nJune 7, 2025November 14, 2025 admin\n\nThe IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR) is one of the top computer vision conferences in the world. The 2025 event will be held in Nashville, starting June 13th.\n\nTo facilitate rapid community engagement with the presented research, we have compiled an extensive index of accepted papers that have associated public code or data repositories. We list all of them in the following table. This index was generated using an automated extraction process. While we strive for completeness, some papers with public resources may have been missed. Please inform us if you discover any additional papers that should be included. Readers should be aware that some code repositories may not be made fully public until the conference officially begins.\n\n#### TABLE 1: CVPR 2025 Papers with Code & Data\n\n | Paper  | Author(s)  | Code\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n1  | Molmo and PixMo: Open Weights and Open Data for State-of-the-Art Vision-Language Models\nRelated Papers   Related Patents   Related Grants   Related Venues   Related Experts   View\nHighlight: Our key contribution is a collection of new dat